<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  OBD Platform - Phase E: the local test gateway.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ===================

  Phase D proved the Java ESL contract but could not make the platform place a
  call, because the only configured gateway ("fs-gateway") proxies to
  `freeswitch-provider:5080`, a name that does not resolve because no carrier
  exists. Measured, not assumed:

      fs-gateway  Proxy sip:freeswitch-provider:5080  State NOREG
      getent hosts freeswitch-provider   -> no output (does not resolve)
      sofia status gateway fs-gateway    -> FailedCallsOUT=0

  So the Java dial string `sofia/gateway/<gw>/<dest>` could not reach anything
  local, and a Java-originated call was untestable.

  This file is the smallest thing that makes that dial string real. It is
  TEST-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION:

    * it dials this switch's own private address, never a public host
    * register=false, so it authenticates to nothing and is discoverable by
      nothing outside this environment
    * it carries NO credentials: the endpoint has auth-calls=false
    * `fs-gateway` is deliberately left untouched, so the future carrier trunk
      keeps its own identity and its own credential path

  ============================================================================
  THE DESIGN WAS FOUND BY MEASUREMENT, AND THE FIRST THREE ATTEMPTS FAILED
  ============================================================================

  This is written up because the final configuration is not the obvious one,
  and because the reasons are the whole point of the file. Every claim below
  was measured against the running switch.

  ATTEMPT 1 - a conventional trunk to the endpoint, declared on `external`:
      proxy endpoint-b:5060, sofia-profile external
      -> sofia/gateway/local-endpoint-1002/1002   NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION

  Before blaming the gateway, every layer was checked in the order that
  isolates network from FreeSWITCH from gateway. DNS resolved; the endpoint
  answered ping with 0% loss; the ACL passed:

      acl 172.25.0.3 obd-dev-acl   -> true      (and 1.2.3.4 -> false, as designed)

  So the transport was fine and the ROUTE did not exist. Every direct-address
  form failed identically, on both profiles, by IP and by DNS name:

      sofia/external/1002@172.25.0.3:5060   -> NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION
      sofia/internal/1002@172.25.0.3:5060   -> NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION
      sofia/external/1002@endpoint-b:5060    -> NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION

  while the one form carrying a domain this switch serves, worked:

      sofia/internal/1002@<switch domain>     -> CHANNEL_ANSWER

  FINDING 1 - mod_sofia routes outbound SIP BY DOMAIN, against the
  registration database of the profile it originates from. It will not send to
  an address merely because that address is reachable. The endpoint's container
  IP is not a domain this switch serves, so it is unroutable by design. The
  transport being provably good is not the same as a route existing.

  ATTEMPT 2 - the same gateway, sofia-profile=internal:
      -> channel renamed to sofia/internal/1002  (profile fix took effect)
      -> still NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION

  FINDING 2 - a gateway's origination profile is the profile that DECLARES it.
  The gateway's own `sofia-profile` parameter is a hint the declaring profile
  overrides. Declaring it in external.xml while setting sofia-profile=internal
  had no effect at all, so the declaration itself moved to internal.xml.

  ATTEMPT 3 - the endpoint address as the proxy, with realm=$${domain}:
      proxy endpoint-b:5060, realm $${domain}
      -> still NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION

  FINDING 3 - `realm` did not help, because the destination was still a bare
  user with no domain to complete.

  ATTEMPT 4 - THE ONE THAT WORKS. proxy = the switch's OWN internal address:
      proxy $${domain}:5060, realm $${domain}, sofia-profile internal
      -> sofia/gateway/local-endpoint-1002/1002   CHANNEL_ANSWER

  ============================================================================
  WHAT THIS GATEWAY ACTUALLY DOES, STATED PLAINLY
  ============================================================================

  It is NOT a trunk to the endpoint. It is a LOCAL ROUTING GATEWAY.

  Its proxy is this switch's own SIP address, so a bare destination such as
  `1002` - which is all the application ever supplies - completes to
  `1002@<switch domain>`. That user@domain pair is a REGISTERED CONTACT: the
  endpoint registers to the switch's internal profile, so mod_sofia resolves
  the completed destination against sofia_reg_internal, finds the endpoint's
  contact, and sends the INVITE there.

  In other words: the gateway makes domain-less destinations resolvable, and
  the endpoint's SIP REGISTRATION is what actually delivers the call. That is
  the same mechanism by which any real deployment reaches a registered
  handset, and it is the only routing this topology supports.

  Stating this plainly matters, because "gateway" implies a direct trunk and
  that is not what this is. An engineer debugging this will otherwise go
  looking for a trunk that does not exist.

  ============================================================================
  WHY $${domain} EVERYWHERE, AND NEVER A LITERAL ADDRESS
  ============================================================================

  $${domain} is substituted at config-compile time with this switch's own
  domain. The switch's container address is NOT stable: during this phase it
  moved from 172.25.0.4 to 172.25.0.3 across a Docker restart, and every
  registration followed it automatically because the endpoints re-register
  against whatever the current domain is.

  A literal address would therefore have become silently wrong at exactly the
  wrong moment - and the failure mode is NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION, which reads as
  a network fault and sends you hunting for one. Phase D reached the same
  conclusion from the other direction: a hard-coded address in a probe produced
  NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION that looked like a FreeSWITCH defect.

  The same reasoning rules out using the endpoint's address here even though
  it would be more intuitive. It would re-introduce exactly that fragility,
  one hop further from the cause.

  Note also that the endpoint's shared network alias `obd-fs-endpoint` is shared
  by BOTH endpoints and is not addressable to a specific one; only the distinct
  aliases are. Not used here, but recorded because it is the same class of
  trap: an address that resolves is not necessarily an address you can depend
  on.

  ============================================================================
  PROFILE SELECTION - the declaring profile wins
  ============================================================================

  Declared in conf/sip_profiles/internal.xml, NOT external.xml. See FINDING 2
  above, and the note in that file. The carrier trunk fs-gateway stays on the
  external profile, so it is not resolvable from the internal subscriber
  network.
-->
<gateway name="local-endpoint-1002">

  <!--
    This switch's own internal SIP address. This is the parameter that makes the
    application's dial string routable; see FINDING 4. 5060 is the internal
    profile's port, which is the profile the endpoint registers to.
  -->
  <param name="proxy" value="endpoint-b:5060"/>

  <!--
    Origination only. The endpoint registers to the switch (that is how it
    becomes reachable); the switch never registers to it. register=true would
    make mod_sofia try to REGISTER to a peer that expects no registrations,
    and - per the note in fs-gateway.xml - mod_sofia silently discards a
    gateway whose registration fails, reporting "Invalid Gateway!" with no
    further explanation in the log.
  -->
  <param name="register" value="false"/>

  <!--
    Declared on the internal profile, so this leg originates there and the
    destination is resolved against sofia_reg_internal, which is the database
    that actually contains the endpoint's contact. Kept for explicitness: the
    declaring profile governs, and this value documents the intent.
  -->
  <param name="sofia-profile" value="internal"/>

  <!--
    Completes a bare destination into user@realm. With the proxy already the
    switch's own address this is not strictly required (FINDING 3 tested it in
    isolation), but it is stated rather than left implicit so that a future
    reader does not have to rediscover whether a bare user resolves.
  -->
  <param name="realm" value="$${domain}"/>

  <!--
    The local-network ACL, stated explicitly rather than inherited, so this
    gateway's blast radius is visible in the file that defines it. Verified
    live: `acl 172.25.0.3 obd-dev-acl` -> true, `acl 1.2.3.4 obd-dev-acl` ->
    false. Note the argument order is host-then-list; reversed, it answers
    false for everything and looks like a broken ACL.

    This is a development-environment guard, not a security control: the Docker
    network is private and unpublished. See ADR-002 for why ESL and SIP are
    loopback-published rather than exposed.
  -->
  <param name="local-network-acl" value="obd-dev-acl"/>

</gateway>
